<% record_url = "https://github.com/hanscanonico/life_simulator/blob/main/docs/design_record.md" %>
<% preregistration_url = "https://github.com/hanscanonico/life_simulator/pull/263" %>
<% window = number_with_delimiter Lab::FromEmergedHeldout::SETTLING_WINDOW, delimiter: " " %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  Rung 3 of the programme asks whether replicators, once they exist, adapt: whether
  selection makes them better at the one thing every replicator does. The
  <%= link_to "copy-cost survey", finding_path("copy-cost-adaptation") %> looked for it in
  every transitioned run and could not separate one lineage improving from a cheaper
  replicator winning. DESIGN §1.3 sweep 11 gives the question a control: it continues an
  emerged world under a treatment and, seed for seed, under its own dynamics. <strong>Under an
  energy economy, does the dominant replicator get faster at copying itself than the same
  world does left alone?</strong>
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Each parent is an economy-off control of sweep 9 whose last stored world is at least half
  replicators by the orientation-aware census. Its terminal world is continued for another
  20 000 epochs under four treatments, three seeds each, paired by (parent, seed): the
  continuation, an economy with influx 2048, a rich economy with influx 8192 (both with steal
  1024, a stock cap of 32 768 and loss 0.5), and host mode.
</p>
<p>
  The reading here is a <strong>held-out confirmatory</strong> one
  (<%= link_to "docs/design_record.md", record_url %>, 2026-09-25, "The from-emerged economy
  arms, confirmed on held-out parents", <%= link_to "#263", preregistration_url %>). Its rules
  were written <strong>after</strong> the sweep's own pre-registered reading
  (2026-09-25, "Runs that start from an emerged world", and its six clarifications) and an
  exploratory look at the children of the first ten parents had been seen, and it is read
  only on the parents that qualified afterwards, none of whose children had been seen.
  For each child that is not extinct, the latency ratio R is its last-decile median
  <span class="mono">copy_latency</span> over its first-decile median, past a settling
  window of <%= window %> epochs. <span class="mono">copy_latency</span> is the step at which a
  noise partner first holds a complete image of the dominant tape, in either orientation.
  A pair favours the treatment where the economy child's R is lower than the continuation
  child's. H3-latency is a one-sided sign test per economy arm at p &lt; 0.05.
</p>

<% reading = @show.from_emerged %>
<h2>Result</h2>
<% if reading&.any? %>
  <p>
    <% if reading.latency_shown? %>
      <span class="badge badge-success">shown</span>
    <% end %>
    <strong><%= reading.claim_sentence %></strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <% reading.latency_tests.each do |test| %>
      <% comparison = test.comparison %>
      <li>
        <%= test.treatment.name %>: <%= comparison.favouring %> of <%= comparison.measured_count %> pairs
        favour the treatment and <%= comparison.against %> the continuation, one-sided sign test
        p = <%= metric_value comparison.p_value.to_f %>,
        <span class="badge <%= test.badge_class %>"><%= test.outcome_label %></span>.
        The treatment wins the majority of its pairs in <%= reading.parents_favouring(test) %> of
        <%= comparison.agreement.size %> held-out parents.
        <% if test.outcome == :held && comparison.carried_by.empty? %>
          No one or two parents carry the result: leaving any of them out keeps p below
          <%= Lab::FromEmergedHeldout::SIGN_TEST_LEVEL %>.
        <% end %>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The economy lowers <span class="mono">copy_latency</span> against the paired continuation,
    on <%= reading.heldout_parent_count %> parents whose children nobody had seen when the rule
    was written. That is selection for faster copying, and it is the programme's first
    confirmed adaptation result.
    <% if reading.interim? %>
      Not every child of the sweep has finished, so this reading is interim.
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <figure class="table-figure mt-4">
    <figcaption class="table-caption text-sm text-muted" id="latency-effect">
      The size of the effect: medians over each treatment's held-out children whose latency is
      measured, in interpreter steps. The ratio is the median of the children's own ratios, not
      the ratio of the two medians beside it.
    </figcaption>
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="latency-effect">
      <table class="data-table" aria-labelledby="latency-effect">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Treatment</th>
            <th class="numeric">Measured</th>
            <th class="numeric">First-decile latency</th>
            <th class="numeric">Last-decile latency</th>
            <th class="numeric">Median ratio R</th>
            <th class="numeric">R below 1</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <% reading.latency_effects.each do |effect| %>
            <tr>
              <td><%= effect.name %></td>
              <td class="numeric"><%= effect.measured %></td>
              <td class="numeric"><%= count_value effect.first_latency %></td>
              <td class="numeric"><%= count_value effect.last_latency %></td>
              <td class="numeric"><%= effect.ratio ? metric_value(effect.ratio.to_f) : "—" %></td>
              <td class="numeric"><%= effect.falling %></td>
            </tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <figure class="table-figure mt-4">
    <figcaption class="table-caption text-sm text-muted" id="latency-agreement">
      H3-latency per held-out parent: the pairs favouring each side.
    </figcaption>
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="latency-agreement">
      <table class="data-table" aria-labelledby="latency-agreement">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Treatment</th>
            <th>Parent</th>
            <th class="numeric">Favour the treatment</th>
            <th class="numeric">Favour the continuation</th>
            <th class="numeric">Tie</th>
            <th class="numeric">Not both measured</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <% reading.latency_tests.each do |test| %>
            <% test.comparison.agreement.each do |row| %>
              <tr>
                <td><%= test.treatment.name %></td>
                <td><%= link_to "run #{row.parent_id}", run_path(row.parent_id) %></td>
                <td class="numeric"><%= row.treatment %></td>
                <td class="numeric"><%= row.continuation %></td>
                <td class="numeric"><%= row.ties %></td>
                <td class="numeric"><%= row.unmeasured %></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    Every count on this page is read from the sweep's stored samples at render time, through
    the same reading <%= link_to "the sweep page", experiment_path("from-emerged", anchor: "heldout-reading") %>
    draws.
  </p>

  <%= render "experiments/heldout_reading", reading: reading.report %>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No held-out child of <%= link_to "the sweep", experiment_path("from-emerged") %> is in this
    database, so there is nothing to read here. This page reads the children at render time
    and states the claim from them.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What is not claimed</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Not in situ.</strong> <span class="mono">copy_latency</span> is measured on the
    world's dominant tape against a fresh noise partner, not in the colony. A lower ratio says
    the dominant copier copies faster in isolation, not that the colony replicates faster.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Survivors only.</strong> An extinct child has no ratio and leaves H3-latency, and a
    treatment that kills the colonies that would not have sped up leaves survivors that did.
    No held-out child went extinct; the settled relapses per treatment are tabled above.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>One substrate, one budget.</strong> Every child is sweep 9's world at caps 128 and
    256, continued for 20 000 epochs, three correlated children per parent.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The mechanism is not identified.</strong> Why copies get faster under an economy —
    a shorter copy loop, a different winner, a tape that spends less before it starts
    copying — is not read here. The ratio is also read only past the settling window, so a
    speed-up inside the first <%= window %> epochs after the switch is not counted.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>No halting copier.</strong> <span class="mono">copy_cost</span>, the steps for one
    byte-exact copy that halts, stays at <span class="mono">max_steps</span> wherever it is
    measured: the copiers loop rather than finish. That is exploratory, not part of the
    pre-registered reading.
  </li>
</ul>
